    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d90">

                <pb facs="0177" n="79" xml:id="pg79"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94 Commission/250: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland (Wilson) to the
                            Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-06-16T16:00:00+01:00">June 16, 1932—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 16—11:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>96. The following letter received from Drummond dated June 15.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“I received last night a telegram from Lytton Commission referring to
                        the reports which have appeared in the press to the effect that there is a
                        strong movement in certain circles in Japan for immediate recognition of the
                        Manchukuo government. The Commission is clearly troubled by this possibility
                        and asks me to ascertain (1) what information is being received from Tokyo
                        by the principal members of the Council on this subject; (2) what action if
                        any could be effective to induce Japan not to take such a step. The
                        Commission observes that any such action by Japan would greatly reduce the
                        prospect of an early friendly settlement of the Manchurian problem and would
                        be difficult to reconcile with the engagement taken by Japan in December ‘to
                        avoid any further aggravation of the situation’.</p>
                    <p>I should be very glad of any information or advice which you could give on
                        the two points specified by the Commission.<anchor n="52" xml:id="d90-f1"
                        /></p>
                    <p>It has asked me to consider its message as nonofficial and confidential. I am
                        writing in a similar sense to the British, French, German and Italian
                        delegations.”</p>
                </quote>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Wilson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d90-f1" n="52" xml:id="d90-f1-content">
                <p>The Department’s telegram No. 87, June 21, 1 p.m., informed the Minister in
                    Switzerland of the contents of telegram No. 161, June 20, noon, from the
                    Ambassador in Japan, <ref target="#pg92">p. 92</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
